# Tofu Milanese

Based on [https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1024089-tofu-milanese](https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1024089-tofu-milanese) ([wayback](http://web.archive.org/web/20251119101652/https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1024089-tofu-milanese))

{ rating="0" image="None" }
:::
:::

## Ingredients

### For breading tofu

- [ ] [Salt and black pepper]{ ing="salt-and-black-pepper" }
- [ ] 1 (14-ounce) package [firm tofu]{ ing="firm-tofu" }
- [ ] ½ cup [all-purpose flour]{ ing="all-purpose-flour" }
- [ ] 1 [egg]{ ing="egg" }
- [ ] ½ cup [whole milk]{ ing="whole-milk" }
- [ ] 2 cups [dried bread crumbs]{ ing="dried-bread-crumbs" }, panko or homemade

### For parsley-caper topping

- [ ] ¼ cup [chopped parsley]{ ing="chopped-parsley" }
- [ ] 1 tablespoon roughly [chopped drained capers]{ ing="chopped-drained-capers" }
- [ ] 1 [lemon]{ ing="lemon" }

### For broccoli rabe

- [ ] 1 bunch [broccoli rabe]{ ing="broccoli-rabe" } (about 1 pound)
- [ ] 2 tablespoons [olive oil]{ ing="olive-oil" }
- [ ] 1 [garlic clove]{ ing="garlic-clove" }, minced
- [ ] Pinch of [red-pepper flakes]{ ing="red-pepper-flakes" }

### Additional

- [ ] 1 cup [olive oil]{ ing="olive-oil" } (for frying, about ½ inch depth)

## Recipe

1. Bring a large pot of well-salted water to a boil. Drain the liquid from the [tofu]{ ing="firm-tofu" } and cut crosswise into 8 rectangular cutlets. Pat dry and season to taste with salt and [pepper]{ ing="salt-and-black-pepper" }.
1. Put [flour]{ ing="all-purpose-flour" } on a plate. Beat together [egg]{ ing="egg" } and [milk]{ ing="whole-milk" } in a shallow medium bowl. Sprinkle [bread crumbs]{ ing="dried-bread-crumbs" } on a rimmed baking sheet.
1. Working one at a time, dip a slice of tofu in the flour, lightly coating all sides. Place in the bowl with egg mixture. Lift slice from egg mixture and set on crumbs in a single layer. Shower the top layer with crumbs and flip to coat well. Repeat with remaining slices. If not using right away, refrigerate, uncovered for up to 4 hours, until ready to cook.
1. To a small bowl, add [parsley]{ ing="chopped-parsley" } and [capers]{ ing="chopped-drained-capers" }, and zest the [lemon]{ ing="lemon" } over. Mix together, and set aside mixture, reserving the rest of the lemon.
1. Add [broccoli rabe]{ ing="broccoli-rabe" } to the boiling water and cook for 1 minute. Drain and cool.
1. Heat 2 tablespoons [olive oil]{ ing="olive-oil" } in a wide 10-inch skillet over medium-high heat. Add garlic and [red pepper]{ ing="red-pepper-flakes" }; let sizzle without browning, about 15 to 30 seconds. Add broccoli rabe and stir to coat. Cook, tossing often, for 3 to 4 minutes, until softened. Pile on a platter.
1. To cook cutlets, wipe out the skillet and, in the same pan, heat ½ inch olive oil (about 1 cup) over medium-high. When oil is wavy, cook the breaded tofu cutlets in 2 batches without crowding until golden and crisp, about 3 minutes per side. Transfer to a paper towel-lined baking sheet and keep warm.
1. To serve, surround the broccoli rabe with tofu cutlets. Stir 2 tablespoons olive oil to parsley-caper mixture. Spoon some onto each cutlet. Slice the lemon into wedges, and serve alongside the cutlet.

## Notes

- Alternatives to broccoli rabe (in order of preference): mustard greens, kale (remove thick stems), Swiss chard, broccolini, regular broccoli, or spinach (reduce cooking time for spinach)
- If capers are not available, substitute with: chopped dill pickles or cornichons (finely diced), chopped green olives, or finely chopped pickled jalapeños for a briny, tangy flavor
